1
00:00:00,128 --> 00:00:03,181
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,279
In today's G C S E Chemistry lesson, we

3
00:00:07,338 --> 00:00:11,088
are going to explore potable water, water treatment

4
00:00:11,262 --> 00:00:15,708
and the processes used to make water safe for human consumption.

5
00:00:16,324 --> 00:00:20,120
We will also examine how waste water is treated and

6
00:00:20,201 --> 00:00:24,299
why obtaining drinking water from seawater can

7
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:25,518
be expensive.

8
00:00:25,855 --> 00:00:28,305
Let us begin with an important question.

9
00:00:28,514 --> 00:00:32,717
Is the water that comes out of your tap chemically pure?

10
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,284
The answer is no.

11
00:00:34,493 --> 00:00:38,405
Although tap water is treated to make it safe to drink,

12
00:00:38,684 --> 00:00:42,852
it still contains small amounts of dissolved substances.

13
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:46,834
In chemistry, we use the term potable water

14
00:00:47,043 --> 00:00:49,841
to describe water that is safe to drink.

15
00:00:50,178 --> 00:00:54,195
Potable water must contain sufficiently low levels

16
00:00:54,346 --> 00:00:58,177
of dissolved salts and harmful microorganisms.

17
00:00:58,456 --> 00:01:01,811
However, it does not need to be chemically pure.

18
00:01:02,055 --> 00:01:05,979
Chemically pure water contains only H two O

19
00:01:06,072 --> 00:01:06,826
molecules.

20
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:10,809
Potable water may contain dissolved minerals, such as

21
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:14,535
calcium and magnesium compounds, provided

22
00:01:14,582 --> 00:01:16,660
their concentrations are safe.

23
00:01:17,113 --> 00:01:20,700
Now, let us consider where our drinking water comes from.

24
00:01:21,037 --> 00:01:24,891
In many parts of the United Kingdom, water is collected

25
00:01:24,973 --> 00:01:28,874
from rivers, lakes, reservoirs and underground

26
00:01:28,955 --> 00:01:29,710
sources.

27
00:01:29,988 --> 00:01:33,796
Before this water can be supplied to homes, it must

28
00:01:33,912 --> 00:01:35,351
undergo treatment.

29
00:01:36,176 --> 00:01:39,856
The exact treatment process depends on the quality of

30
00:01:39,937 --> 00:01:41,052
the source water.

31
00:01:41,667 --> 00:01:44,338
Let us examine some of the main stages.

32
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:47,600
The first stage often involves screening.

33
00:01:48,053 --> 00:01:52,093
Water passes through screens that remove large objects such

34
00:01:52,139 --> 00:01:54,891
as leaves, branches and other debris.

35
00:01:55,506 --> 00:02:00,115
Next, suspended particles may be removed by sedimentation.

36
00:02:00,638 --> 00:02:04,283
During sedimentation, water is allowed to stand or

37
00:02:04,364 --> 00:02:08,277
flow slowly through a tank so that heavier suspended

38
00:02:08,359 --> 00:02:10,390
particles settle at the bottom.

39
00:02:10,843 --> 00:02:14,535
In many treatment plants, chemicals are added to help

40
00:02:14,616 --> 00:02:18,750
small suspended particles clump together into larger particles

41
00:02:18,901 --> 00:02:20,816
that can settle more easily.

42
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:25,471
This process is called coagulation and flocculation.

43
00:02:26,087 --> 00:02:29,837
The water may then pass through filters containing materials

44
00:02:29,883 --> 00:02:31,660
such as sand and gravel.

45
00:02:31,996 --> 00:02:36,118
Filtration removes remaining suspended particles and

46
00:02:36,234 --> 00:02:40,344
helps reduce the amount of unwanted material in the water.

47
00:02:40,739 --> 00:02:44,535
However, filtration alone does not necessarily

48
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:47,786
remove all harmful microorganisms.

49
00:02:48,111 --> 00:02:51,803
For this reason, the water must also be disinfected.

50
00:02:52,244 --> 00:02:54,729
One common method is chlorination.

51
00:02:55,054 --> 00:02:59,257
During chlorination, a carefully controlled amount of chlorine

52
00:02:59,408 --> 00:03:04,121
is added to kill or inactivate harmful microorganisms.

53
00:03:05,096 --> 00:03:09,021
Other disinfection methods include ultraviolet radiation and

54
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:10,367
ozone treatment.

55
00:03:10,983 --> 00:03:14,663
The treated water is then tested to ensure that it meets the

56
00:03:14,733 --> 00:03:18,935
required drinking-water quality standards before distribution.

57
00:03:19,144 --> 00:03:21,988
Now, let us consider waste water.

58
00:03:22,232 --> 00:03:26,191
Waste water includes water that has been used in homes,

59
00:03:26,365 --> 00:03:28,293
businesses and industries.

60
00:03:28,571 --> 00:03:32,623
Sewage is a form of waste water that may contain human

61
00:03:32,716 --> 00:03:37,244
waste, food residues, detergents and microorganisms.

62
00:03:37,638 --> 00:03:41,946
Before it can be safely discharged into the environment, sewage

63
00:03:42,062 --> 00:03:43,223
must be treated.

64
00:03:43,559 --> 00:03:46,822
Let us examine the main stages of sewage treatment.

65
00:03:47,344 --> 00:03:50,618
First, large objects are removed by screening.

66
00:03:50,897 --> 00:03:55,041
The sewage then passes through tanks where solids can settle.

67
00:03:55,436 --> 00:03:59,453
This process separates the sewage into a liquid component,

68
00:03:59,662 --> 00:04:03,505
called effluent, and a solid-rich component, called

69
00:04:03,749 --> 00:04:04,492
sludge.

70
00:04:04,829 --> 00:04:08,079
The sludge may undergo anaerobic digestion.

71
00:04:08,416 --> 00:04:12,538
During this process, microorganisms break down organic

72
00:04:12,631 --> 00:04:15,103
matter in the absence of oxygen.

73
00:04:15,347 --> 00:04:19,678
One useful product is biogas, which contains methane

74
00:04:19,794 --> 00:04:21,710
and can be used as a fuel.

75
00:04:21,837 --> 00:04:26,063
The liquid effluent undergoes further biological treatment.

76
00:04:26,272 --> 00:04:31,195
In aerobic treatment, air is supplied so that microorganisms

77
00:04:31,438 --> 00:04:35,513
can break down dissolved and suspended organic matter.

78
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:40,355
Further settling and additional treatment may then take place

79
00:04:40,413 --> 00:04:44,616
before the treated effluent is discharged or reused.

80
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:48,889
The exact treatment stages depend on the treatment

81
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:51,745
works and the required water quality.

82
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:55,500
Now, let us examine another important source of

83
00:04:55,581 --> 00:04:57,149
water: seawater.

84
00:04:57,392 --> 00:05:01,317
Seawater contains a high concentration of dissolved

85
00:05:01,409 --> 00:05:05,322
salts, making it unsuitable for ordinary drinking

86
00:05:05,415 --> 00:05:06,460
without treatment.

87
00:05:06,796 --> 00:05:10,558
The process of removing dissolved salts from seawater

88
00:05:10,767 --> 00:05:12,810
is called desalination.

89
00:05:13,205 --> 00:05:16,189
One method of desalination is distillation.

90
00:05:16,642 --> 00:05:20,589
During distillation, seawater is heated so that water

91
00:05:20,705 --> 00:05:21,750
evaporates.

92
00:05:22,203 --> 00:05:25,907
The water vapour is then cooled and condensed to produce

93
00:05:25,953 --> 00:05:26,859
liquid water.

94
00:05:27,195 --> 00:05:30,806
Most dissolved salts remain behind because they do not

95
00:05:30,887 --> 00:05:34,057
evaporate with the water under these conditions.

96
00:05:34,579 --> 00:05:37,307
Another method is reverse osmosis.

97
00:05:37,551 --> 00:05:41,684
In reverse osmosis, high pressure forces water through

98
00:05:41,742 --> 00:05:45,469
a specialised membrane that allows water molecules to pass

99
00:05:45,806 --> 00:05:48,441
while rejecting most dissolved salts.

100
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:53,085
Both methods can produce water suitable for drinking after

101
00:05:53,166 --> 00:05:55,709
any necessary additional treatment.

102
00:05:55,987 --> 00:05:59,807
However, desalination is often more expensive than treating

103
00:05:59,877 --> 00:06:00,712
freshwater.

104
00:06:01,165 --> 00:06:01,746
Why?

105
00:06:02,361 --> 00:06:05,983
Distillation requires substantial energy to heat and

106
00:06:06,053 --> 00:06:07,307
evaporate water.

107
00:06:07,922 --> 00:06:11,568
Reverse osmosis requires energy to generate the

108
00:06:11,649 --> 00:06:15,190
high pressure needed to force water through the membrane.

109
00:06:15,898 --> 00:06:19,694
Desalination plants also require specialised equipment,

110
00:06:19,903 --> 00:06:23,735
maintenance and careful management of concentrated salt

111
00:06:23,804 --> 00:06:24,338
waste.

112
00:06:24,954 --> 00:06:27,798
Now, let us work through an example.

113
00:06:28,193 --> 00:06:31,885
Imagine that water is collected from a river for use

114
00:06:31,931 --> 00:06:33,138
as drinking water.

115
00:06:33,533 --> 00:06:37,202
The water may contain leaves, suspended soil particles

116
00:06:37,318 --> 00:06:38,932
and microorganisms.

117
00:06:39,210 --> 00:06:42,020
First, screening removes large debris.

118
00:06:42,357 --> 00:06:46,374
Next, sedimentation and filtration remove suspended

119
00:06:46,432 --> 00:06:47,233
particles.

120
00:06:47,511 --> 00:06:51,424
Finally, disinfection reduces harmful microorganisms

121
00:06:51,470 --> 00:06:52,527
to safe levels.

122
00:06:52,701 --> 00:06:56,091
The water is tested before being supplied to consumers.

123
00:06:56,335 --> 00:07:00,189
Notice that different treatment stages remove different types

124
00:07:00,247 --> 00:07:01,443
of contamination.

125
00:07:01,652 --> 00:07:05,809
This is why a combination of processes is often necessary.

126
00:07:06,052 --> 00:07:07,887
Let us test your understanding.

127
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:09,791
What is potable water?

128
00:07:10,035 --> 00:07:12,043
Water that is safe to drink.

129
00:07:12,124 --> 00:07:15,085
Does potable water have to be chemically pure?

130
00:07:15,329 --> 00:07:15,782
No.

131
00:07:15,956 --> 00:07:19,764
It may contain small amounts of dissolved substances.

132
00:07:20,008 --> 00:07:24,083
Which treatment process uses chlorine to kill or inactivate

133
00:07:24,141 --> 00:07:25,801
harmful microorganisms?

134
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:27,241
Chlorination.

135
00:07:27,856 --> 00:07:31,293
And what is the name of the process used to remove dissolved

136
00:07:31,351 --> 00:07:32,976
salts from seawater?

137
00:07:33,684 --> 00:07:34,834
Desalination.

138
00:07:35,356 --> 00:07:39,466
Now, let us tackle a G C S E examination question.

139
00:07:39,861 --> 00:07:43,727
Explain why desalination is often more expensive than obtaining

140
00:07:43,808 --> 00:07:46,513
potable water from freshwater sources.

141
00:07:46,722 --> 00:07:48,893
This question is worth four marks.

142
00:07:49,137 --> 00:07:52,759
A model answer would be: Seawater contains high

143
00:07:52,818 --> 00:07:56,556
concentrations of dissolved salts that must be removed

144
00:07:56,591 --> 00:07:58,739
before it can be used as drinking water.

145
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:02,558
Desalination by distillation requires large amounts

146
00:08:02,593 --> 00:08:05,531
of energy to heat and evaporate the water.

147
00:08:05,739 --> 00:08:09,791
Reverse osmosis also requires energy to generate

148
00:08:09,849 --> 00:08:11,962
high pressure across a membrane.

149
00:08:12,055 --> 00:08:15,782
These processes require specialised equipment and operating

150
00:08:15,863 --> 00:08:19,822
costs, making desalination generally more expensive

151
00:08:19,869 --> 00:08:22,284
than conventional freshwater treatment.

152
00:08:22,737 --> 00:08:26,765
Notice that the answer identifies the problem, explains

153
00:08:26,858 --> 00:08:30,701
the scientific processes involved and links them to the higher

154
00:08:30,771 --> 00:08:31,189
cost.

155
00:08:31,468 --> 00:08:35,009
Before we finish, remember these important points.

156
00:08:35,183 --> 00:08:39,142
Potable water is safe to drink but is not necessarily

157
00:08:39,316 --> 00:08:40,489
chemically pure.

158
00:08:40,732 --> 00:08:44,552
Freshwater treatment commonly involves filtration and

159
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:45,492
disinfection.

160
00:08:45,771 --> 00:08:49,718
Sewage treatment separates solids from liquids and uses

161
00:08:49,834 --> 00:08:53,132
biological processes to remove organic matter.

162
00:08:53,526 --> 00:08:57,485
Desalination removes dissolved salts from seawater,

163
00:08:57,532 --> 00:09:00,260
but it can require considerable energy.

